-- title:
--   Uniqueness of the place with ̃ j polar and ̃ jₚ̃ j⁻ᵖ vanishing
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and $p$ a prime. Inside the Laurent series field $K((q))$ consider the element $\tilde j := \mathtt{jqModC}\,K = q^{-1}\cdot \sum_n a_n q^n$, the image in $K((q))$ of the integral power series $E_4^3\cdot \eta^{-1}$-type numerator `jNum` shifted by $q^{-1}$, and its $q\mapsto q^p$ substitute $\tilde j_p := \mathtt{jqNModC}\,K\,p$, obtained by applying the degree-scaling ring homomorphism $\mathtt{qExpand}\,K\,p$ to $\tilde j$; let $F := K(\tilde j,\tilde j_p)$ be the intermediate field of $K((q))/K$ generated by these two elements. A place of $F$ over $K$ is, by definition, a valuation subring of $F$ containing $\mathrm{im}(K\to F)$, distinct from $F$ itself, and a principal ideal ring; for such a place $u$ and $f\in F$, $u.\mathrm{ord}\,f$ denotes the integer valuation attached to the corresponding height-one prime. The assertion is that for any two places $u,u'$ of $F$ over $K$ such that $\mathrm{ord}_u \tilde j<0$ and $\mathrm{ord}_u(\tilde j_p\cdot(\tilde j^{-1})^p)>0$, and likewise $\mathrm{ord}_{u'} \tilde j<0$ and $\mathrm{ord}_{u'}(\tilde j_p\cdot(\tilde j^{-1})^p)>0$, one has $u=u'$.
--
--   This is the uniqueness half of the description of the cusps of the level-$p$ modular curve in the affine chart with coordinates $\tilde s=\tilde j^{-1}$ and $\tilde t=\tilde j_p\tilde j^{-p}$: at most one place of $K(\tilde j,\tilde j_p)$ lies at $\tilde s=\tilde t=0$, a statement valid over an arbitrary base field, in particular in characteristic $p$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.cuspRuleStrongFor`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.cuspRuleStrongFor) in the analysis of places of the modular function field and their specialisations.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_cuspChartInftyZero_place_unique.lean

